{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2272 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "01dc725e955eb2a0fd1cf4d194392eec03326d2511137706e3fadfddf3ad7fd4" :edb/db-before-t 2271 :edb/db-after-t 2272 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535584 50 #inst "2026-10-09T02:59:31.797Z" 13194139535584 true] [17592186133802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356152 13194139535584 true] [17592186133813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356145 13194139535584 true] [17592186133813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356159 13194139535584 true] [17592186133813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356166 13194139535584 true] [17592186134138 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535584 true] [17592186134138 1100 "Consider the vector field $F$, defined as\n\n$F(x, y) = \\frac{x}{1 + x^{2} + y^{2}}i + \\frac{y}{1 + x^{2} + y^{2}}j$.\n\nEvaluate $∫_{C}F \\cdot dr$, where $C$ is the curve $y = x^{2}\\ln (x + 1)$ for $x ∈ [0, 1]$." 13194139535584 false] [17592186134138 1100 "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ for $x\\in [0,1].$" 13194139535584 true] [17592186134138 1145 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{1}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356145 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356146 13194139535584 true] [17592186356145 1089 "q-104626" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356145 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535584 true] [17592186356145 1100 "Let $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [-1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356145 1113 #uuid "9fd89d80-4cad-5b98-abe3-ec15ead33c00" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356145 1145 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [-1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(2,8).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(-1)) \\\\  & =f(2,8)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}+{8}^{3})-\\frac{1}{3}((-1)^{3}+{2}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(520)-\\frac{1}{3}(7) \\\\  & =\\frac{520}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =171.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356146 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356147 13194139535584 true] [17592186356146 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356148 13194139535584 true] [17592186356146 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356149 13194139535584 true] [17592186356146 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356150 13194139535584 true] [17592186356146 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356151 13194139535584 true] [17592186356146 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535584 true] [17592186356146 1041 "selection" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356146 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356151 13194139535584 true] [17592186356146 1110 #uuid "9220d2fe-04da-50b9-bba9-6f422d1ff1bb" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356147 1034 "213" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356147 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535584 true] [17592186356147 1111 #uuid "25dadb3a-0600-58bf-8a51-1c6b0e5a4b94" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356148 1034 "198" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356148 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535584 true] [17592186356148 1111 #uuid "57e84b56-0713-5e59-99f6-f53c7b9e107a" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356149 1034 "65"