{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-e⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,9-{e}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-e)$ and $(3,9-{e}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,9-{e}^{2})-f(0,e) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {3}^{3}-3\\cdot (9-{e}^{2}))-(2\\cdot {0}^{3}-0\\cdot (-e)) \\\\  & =(54-27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =(27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3({e}^{2}+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356153 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356154 13194139535584 true] [17592186356153 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356155 13194139535584 true] [17592186356153 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356156 13194139535584 true] [17592186356153 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356157 13194139535584 true] [17592186356153 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356158 13194139535584 true] [17592186356153 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535584 true] [17592186356153 1041 "selection" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356153 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356157 13194139535584 true] [17592186356153 1110 #uuid "eb853897-cc12-5400-8a93-6553a2ba66ca" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356154 1034 "3{e}^{2}-1" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356154 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535584 true] [17592186356154 1111 #uuid "324d0659-5d73-5aa6-87ca-7874cf9a4d40" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356155 1034 "{e}^{2}+27" 13194139535584 true] [17592186356155 1103 #edb/ref 1759218604